+# SMS sign-in integration
+
+The homepage is static. Live SMS delivery and session creation require two
+same-origin HTTPS endpoints. The UI deliberately does not accept a code or claim
+an SMS was sent until the server confirms it.
+
+## Send
+
+`POST /api/auth/sms/send`
+
+Request: `{ "phone": "+15551234567" }`
+
+Success: `{ "challengeId": "opaque-server-generated-id" }`
+
+Send a six-digit code bound to that challenge and phone number. Format the final
+line of the SMS with the exact hostname serving this page (no scheme or path):
+
+```text
+Your Daily Grind verification code is 123456.
+
+@your-hostname.example #123456
+```
+
+The page starts WebOTP listening before requesting delivery, when supported in a
+secure context. It also sets `autocomplete="one-time-code"` for system autofill.
+Manual entry and paste work without WebOTP. SMS reading remains subject to the
+browser's permission flow; filling the code never submits the form automatically.
+
+## Verify
+
+`POST /api/auth/sms/verify`
+
+Request: `{ "challengeId": "opaque-server-generated-id", "phone": "+15551234567", "code": "123456" }`
+
+Success: `{ "verified": true }`, with the authenticated session set in a Secure,
+HttpOnly, SameSite cookie. The page then navigates to `/rewards` or `/order-online`
+according to the selected purpose. Those destinations must enforce authentication.
+
+Return a non-success HTTP status for failures: 400/401/410 for invalid or expired
+codes and 429 for rate limits. Validate and expire codes on the server, consume
+successful challenges once, bind them to the requesting session and number, and
+limit send and verification attempts. Check request origins and apply the app's
+CSRF protections. Never return or log plaintext codes. The 30-second UI resend
+countdown is only a convenience; enforce limits server-side.
+
+Browser behavior reference: https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/WebOTP_API
